**Claim.** The supremum of $g(1)=\int_0^1\|f'(s)\|\,ds$ over all $C^1$ maps $f:[0,1]\to[0,1]\times[0,1]$ is $+\infty$. In particular, $g(1)$ is unbounded above and no finite maximum is attained; the maximum possible value is $\boxed{+\infty}$.

**Proof.** It suffices to exhibit, for every positive integer $N$, a $C^1$ curve $f_N:[0,1]\to[0,1]^2$ with $g_N(1)\ge N$.

For each $N\ge 1$ define
$$
f_N(t)=\Bigl(t,\;\tfrac12+\tfrac14\sin(2\pi N t)\Bigr),\qquad t\in[0,1].
$$

*Well-definedness and the box constraint.* Since $0\le t\le 1$ and $\sin$ takes values in $[-1,1]$, the second coordinate satisfies $\tfrac14\le \tfrac12+\tfrac14\sin(2\pi N t)\le \tfrac34$. Hence $f_N(t)\in[0,1]\times[\tfrac14,\tfrac34]\subset[0,1]\times[0,1]$ for all $t$. The map $f_N$ is $C^1$ (in fact $C^\infty$).

*Derivative.* 
$$
f_N'(t)=\Bigl(1,\;\tfrac{\pi N}{2}\cos(2\pi N t)\Bigr),
$$
so
$$
\|f_N'(t)\|=\sqrt{1+\tfrac{\pi^2 N^2}{4}\cos^2(2\pi N t)}\;\ge\;\tfrac{\pi N}{2}\,|\cos(2\pi N t)|.
$$

*Lower bound on arc length.* Integrating,
$$
g_N(1)=\int_0^1\|f_N'(t)\|\,dt\;\ge\;\frac{\pi N}{2}\int_0^1|\cos(2\pi N t)|\,dt.
$$
The function $|\cos(2\pi N t)|$ has period $1/(2N)$ in $t$, so over $[0,1]$ it completes $2N$ full periods. On one period,
$$
\int_0^{1/(2N)}|\cos(2\pi N t)|\,dt=\frac{1}{2\pi N}\int_0^{\pi}|\cos u|\,du=\frac{1}{2\pi N}\cdot 2=\frac{1}{\pi N}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\int_0^1|\cos(2\pi N t)|\,dt=2N\cdot\frac{1}{\pi N}=\frac{2}{\pi},
$$
and consequently
$$
g_N(1)\;\ge\;\frac{\pi N}{2}\cdot\frac{2}{\pi}=N.
$$

*Conclusion.* Since for every positive integer $N$ there exists a $C^1$ map $f_N:[0,1]\to[0,1]\times[0,1]$ with $g_N(1)\ge N$, the quantity $g(1)$ can be made arbitrarily large. Hence
$$
\sup_{f\in C^1([0,1],[0,1]^2)} g(1)=+\infty,
$$
and the supremum is not attained by any finite value. The maximum possible value of $g(1)$ is $+\infty$. $\blacksquare$

